---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
// Rendered by the Worker, not prerendered: a static file is served before the middleware, so the old host
// mcp-pulse.ulehla.dev would answer 200 instead of the 301. The page reads no data; the cache keeps it a day.
Astro.response.headers.set('cache-control', 'public, max-age=3600, s-maxage=86400');
---

<Layout title="Methodology" description="How mcpcost probes every MCP server in the official registry each day and counts the context tokens its tool list costs, exactly for Claude and Gemini.">
  <p class="kicker">method</p>
  <h1 class="poster">How mcpcost measures</h1>
  <p class="lede">One open-source probe. The same steps for every server.</p>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="what-we-probe-h">
    <h2 id="what-we-probe-h" class="panel-title">What we probe</h2>
  <p>
    The probe reads the <a href="https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io" rel="noopener">official MCP registry</a>. It uses the latest version of each server.
    It takes the first <code>streamable-http</code> remote. If the server has only an <code>sse</code> remote, it takes that one.
    Servers that ship only as a package on npm or PyPI are probed too: the package is installed in a container with the network on and no lifecycle scripts, then started with the network off and placeholder values for the environment variables the registry entry lists. “needs env”, “needs args” and “needs network” mean the server started but wants a real credential, an argument or a network. “crashed on start”, “install failed” and “no executable” are what they say. OCI and MCPB packages are not probed.
  </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="how-we-probe-a-server-h">
    <h2 id="how-we-probe-a-server-h" class="panel-title">How we probe a server</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Connect with the official TypeScript SDK. The client name is <code>mcpcost</code>. We send no credentials, with one exception: a server whose owner stored credentials through <code>mcpcost auth</code> is probed with them, and its page says “measured with credentials”.</li>
    <li>Send <code>initialize</code> and <code>notifications/initialized</code>. Record the time to the <code>initialize</code> result, the protocol version, the server name and version, the capabilities and the length of the instructions.</li>
    <li>If the server has tools, call <code>tools/list</code>. Follow the pagination. Record the time and the tools.</li>
    <li>Do not open the standalone GET stream. The probe answers 405 to the SDK's GET. The probe needs no messages from the server. Some servers stop <code>tools/list</code> while that stream is open.</li>
    <li>Close the session.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Each request has a timeout of 15 seconds. We run one probe at a time against each host.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="results-h">
    <h2 id="results-h" class="panel-title">Results</h2>
  <dl class="facts">
    <dt>alive</dt><dd><code>initialize</code> and <code>tools/list</code> were successful.</dd>
    <dt>needs auth</dt><dd id="access">HTTP 401 or 403. We record the <code>WWW-Authenticate</code> scheme when the server sends one. The server is reachable, but it does not talk to clients without credentials. We then read what the server publishes about getting in, never a token: the OAuth protected-resource metadata (RFC 9728, the URL from <code>WWW-Authenticate</code> or the well-known path) gives the authorization servers, the scopes and the publisher's guide; the first authorization server's metadata (RFC 8414, then OpenID discovery) says whether clients can register themselves (RFC 7591) and whether PKCE S256 is supported. The server page sums it up as one of four kinds: OAuth where clients register themselves, OAuth where the publisher issues the client id, a key or token from the publisher, or nothing published. Two to four small requests, 10 seconds each; the probe result does not depend on them.</dd>
    <dt>paid (402)</dt><dd>HTTP 402 Payment Required.</dd>
    <dt>newer protocol</dt><dd>The server answers with a protocol version that the current SDK does not support. The server is reachable.</dd>
    <dt>not found</dt><dd>HTTP 404, 405 or 410 on the MCP endpoint.</dd>
    <dt>not MCP</dt><dd>HTTP 200, but the response is not a valid MCP reply. Or the server returned a JSON-RPC error to <code>initialize</code>.</dd>
    <dt>timeout, no DNS, TLS error, refused, server error, rate limited</dt><dd>Transport failures. We name each one after its cause.</dd>
  </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="tokens-h" id="tokens">
    <h2 id="tokens-h" class="panel-title">Token counts</h2>
  <p>
    A server page leads with one number: the exact Claude count when the run has it, else the estimate. The facts under it hold the other counts, and a server that needs auth can show the tool set of its publisher's open-source package instead (see below).
  </p>
  <dl class="facts">
    <dt>Claude</dt><dd>The exact count from the Anthropic <code>count_tokens</code> endpoint, model <code>claude-opus-5-5</code>, tool names as the server publishes them. We send one request with the server's tools and a small marker tool, and one request with the marker tool only. The difference is what the server's tools cost. The tool-use system prompt is in both requests, so it falls out. The endpoint is free and calls no model.</dd>
    <dt>Gemini</dt><dd>The same method against the Gemini <code>countTokens</code> endpoint, model <code>gemini-3.8-flash</code>, with each tool sent as a function declaration with its JSON Schema as it is (<code>parametersJsonSchema</code>). Present on a server page once a run had a Gemini key. The endpoint is free and calls no model.</dd>
    <dt>Claude Code</dt><dd>The same count with the prefix <code>mcp__&lt;server&gt;__</code> on each tool name, as Claude Code sends it. The server part is the last segment of the registry name. In your own config it is the key you chose, so your number can differ by a few tokens.</dd>
    <dt>o200k</dt><dd>An estimate. We serialize each tool as compact JSON with <code>name</code>, <code>description</code> and <code>input_schema</code> and count with the <code>o200k_base</code> tokenizer. It is the same estimate for every server and it needs no API key. The exact Claude count is about 1.9 times this estimate at the median, because Claude renders the schemas in its own format and tokenizes prose in its own way.</dd>
  </dl>
  <p>
    “Of a 200k context window” divides the Claude count by 200 000. A request with tools also carries a tool-use system prompt one time: 286 tokens for Claude Opus 5.5. We do not add it to any server. Claude Code defers MCP tool definitions by default (tool search) and loads a tool's definition when it needs it; the count is then the ceiling, not what every turn pays.
  </p>
  <p>
    We count a server again only when its tool definitions change. The date next to the Claude count is the date of that count. Some servers publish a schema the endpoint refuses, for example a top-level oneOf or two tools with the same name; they show the estimate and the reason. These servers show the estimate and the reason.
  </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="same-tool-set-h">
    <h2 id="same-tool-set-h" class="panel-title">Same tool set</h2>
  <p>
    We hash the sorted tool names and their schema sizes. Servers with the same hash serve the same tools. This shows bulk publishers that register one server under many names.
  </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="live-check-h">
    <h2 id="live-check-h" class="panel-title">Live check</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/check">/check</a> probes one server on demand. It uses the same steps and a timeout of 10 seconds. The Worker does the network part and asks <code>count_tokens</code> for the exact Claude count. Your browser counts the o200k estimate, because the Worker has a small CPU budget. We keep a result for ten minutes. A URL that is already in the registry opens its page.
  </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="gated-h">
    <h2 id="gated-h" class="panel-title">Servers that need auth</h2>
    <p>
      A server that answers 401 or 402 is reachable but gives no tool list. Two things fill the gap. The owner of the board can store credentials for a server with <code>mcpcost auth</code> (the MCP OAuth flow or a header); the daily probe then uses them and the page says “measured with credentials”. For the rest, the tool definitions do not depend on the token: when the registry entry names a public repository with a package on npm or PyPI, the package is started in the container like any package server, and the page shows its tool set with the note “from the publisher's package”.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="tracked-h">
    <h2 id="tracked-h" class="panel-title">Outside the registry</h2>
    <p>
      A URL that is not in the registry can be tracked from <a href="/check">/check</a>. The daily probe then measures it with the registry servers, under the name <code>url/&lt;host&gt;/&lt;path&gt;</code>, with a page, a badge, a feed and alerts like any other server.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="retries-h">
    <h2 id="retries-h" class="panel-title">Second attempts</h2>
    <p>
      A server that times out but answered in the previous run gets a second, slower attempt at the end of the run, so a fault on our side does not show as an outage. A package server that exits asking for a variable is started once more with that variable set to a placeholder.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="machine-access-h">
    <h2 id="machine-access-h" class="panel-title">Machine access</h2>
  <p>
    Each server has JSON at <code>/api/s/&lt;name&gt;.json</code>. The list is at <code>/api/servers.json</code>. The live probe is at <code>/api/check.json?url=…</code>. The run summary is at <code>/api/summary.json</code>.
    The board is also an MCP server: <code>https://mcpcost.ulehla.dev/mcp</code> over Streamable HTTP, no auth. Its tools are <code>lookup_server</code>, <code>search_servers</code>, <code>check_server</code> and <code>registry_summary</code>. <a href="/llms.txt">/llms.txt</a> lists all of this.
    The newest run as re-check evidence for the registry, in the row shape agreed in <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/registry/issues/1715" rel="noopener">registry issue #1715</a>, is at <code>/data/mcpcost-evidence-latest.json.gz</code>. It changes after each daily run, and <code>/data/mcpcost-evidence-latest.index.json</code> gives its run date, number of rows and size.
  </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="being-a-good-guest-h">
    <h2 id="being-a-good-guest-h" class="panel-title">Being a good guest</h2>
  <p>
    Each request carries the User-Agent <code>mcpcost/&lt;version&gt; (+https://github.com/ondraulehla/mcpcost)</code>. The probe runs one time each day. It sends no tool calls. It makes at most three requests to each server, six when the first attempt timed out and the server answered the day before.
    To exclude a server, <a href="https://github.com/ondraulehla/mcpcost/issues" rel="noopener">open an issue</a> with its registry name.
  </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel" aria-labelledby="run-it-yourself-h">
    <h2 id="run-it-yourself-h" class="panel-title">Run it yourself</h2>
  <pre>git clone https://github.com/ondraulehla/mcpcost
cd mcpcost && npm install
npm run probe -- --sample 200 --per-host 2</pre>
  </section>
</Layout>
